{
  "extends": "../config/tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "noEmit": true,
    "types": ["node"],
    /*
      DECLARATION EMIT IS OFF HERE, AND IT IS NOT A WORKAROUND.

      The shared base turns `declaration` on, which makes `tsc` enforce that
      every exported type can be written down by a portable path, even when
      `noEmit` means no declaration file is ever produced. Every package in
      this workspace is consumed as SOURCE: `exports` on each one points at
      `./src/index.ts`, nothing builds a `.d.ts`, and none exists anywhere in
      the tree. So the constraint has no consumer here.

      What it does have is a cost. The route registry is one object naming
      several hundred handlers, so its inferred type touches a type from
      nearly every service, and any one of them mentioning a type from deep
      inside another package fails the whole registry with TS2742: an error in
      a file nobody edited, naming a path nobody wrote.

      If this package ever ships compiled declarations, turn this back on and
      fix the leaks properly: the fix is an explicit return or parameter type
      at each boundary, which `services/labor.ts` already does in two places
      and says why.
    */
    "declaration": false
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "test/**/*.ts"]
}
